  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Atomic number of Cu is 29 .\r\nThus, its electronic configuration can be written as\r\n$$\r\n\\mathrm{Cu}_{29}=1s^{2}, 2 s^{2}, 2 p^{6}, 3 s^{2}, 3 p^{6}, 3 d^{10}, 4 s^{1}\r\n$$\r\n(Since, fully filled orbitals are more stable, so one electron from 4 s orbital transfers into $3 d$ orbital.\r\nThe electronic configuration of neon is\r\n$$\r\n\\mathrm{Ne}_{10}=1 \\mathrm{s}^{2}, 2 s^{2}, 2 p^{6}\r\n$$\r\nThus, using inert gas, the configuration of Cu can also be represented as\r\n$$\r\n\\mathrm{Cu}_{29}=[\\mathrm{Ne}] 3 \\mathrm{s}^{2}, 3 p^{6}, 3 d^{10}, 4 \\mathrm{s}^{1}\r\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
